  *** Revision History ***

  Version 1.0á - May 5, 1996
   . First beta release.

  Version 1.1á - May 21, 1996
   . Fixed TYPE problem. (%s's would show up as <NULL POINTERS>)
   . Fixed CHKDSK problem, chkdsk would only work on the current drive
     that you were on.

  Version 1.2á - May 22, 1996
   . Fixed problem with GP's happening when files were uploaded.

  Version 1.3á - May 28, 1996
   . Fixed problem with CHKDSK not working, as well as volume labels
     being printed as "the last file accessed..".  This was all linked
     to the fact that some hard drives had "no label."  This problem
     should *hopefully* be fixed now!
   . Someone reported a problem with copying files from networked drives.
     We have successfully backed up our WHOLE bbs onto a network drive,
     so we don't have this problem (using DS-DOS).  If anyone else
     runs into a similar error, please let me know!

  Version 1.4á - June 1, 1996
   . Fixed the .MSG block error for help on the MOVE command.  Instead
     of showing the help for MOVE it showed the help for COPY.
   . Added the saving of your working environment!  If you left DS-DOS
     in the C:\MYDIR\IS\COOL directory, next time you enter DS-DOS
     you will return to that directory with all of your DOSKEY history
     remembered!  This will work even if you logoff the system, and
     call back in 25 years!
   . Added commands:
     history - will display all commands in your DOSKEY history.
     clear   - will clear all the commands in your DOSKEY history.

  Version 1.5á - June 6, 1996
   . Special fix for Neil's BBS.  Couldn't change directories because
     there was a NULL device in his BIOS table. Fixed!

  Version 1.6á - June 13, 1996
   . Fixed a little error on my part (ack!) for copying files to the
     root directory.  If you were at C:\ and typed:
       copy \somedir\filename.txt
     It would reply: "Path not found- C:\*.*\"
   . Fixed an error where directories on Netware volumes couldn't be
     created.  This is caused by Netware's file system. Even if the
     directory didn't exist, it would say it did.  Fixed!
   . Added the DELTREE command!  Yes, a very powerful command indeed.
     It should work 100% fine.  It will not let you deltree the root
     directory of any drive at all, this is for your safety.
   . Added the SPEED command.  The speed command will allow you to
     alter the rate at which files copy on your system!  Each copy
     cycle, the number of bytes copied equals: 256*Current speed.

     The default speed is 50: Which means 10,240 bytes are copied
     per cycle.  Each speed is independent of the user. Your current
     speed is saved with your environment, so if you set your speed
     to 75, the next time you come in your speed will be 75!

     For information, here's a benchmark I did on my NEC Versa notebook.
     (486dx2-50 processor):

     Command: XCOPY C:\PROWIN3 C:\RICHIE /S

     Total copied bytes: 35,009,504 (35 megs!)

       Speed ==   2 : 10 minutes (512 bytes per cycle)
       Speed == 100 :  7 minutes (25,600 bytes per cycle)

     Note: High speeds may cause your server to lose response time!
           Test it out to see the optimal copy speed.

   . Fixed an error with XCOPY.  If you tried to XCOPY a directory which
     had no files, but had subdirectories XCOPY would tell you that the
     directory is empty.  Fixed!
   . Added some usage to the MEM command.  Will now tell you the amount
     of high memory available, as well as the current VDA (Volatile Data
     Area) settings. I don't know what possible use this command would
     bring anyone, but someone requested it, so here it is!

  Version 1.7á - June 15, 1996
   . Several people have left us messages saying DS-DOS isn't deleting
     certain files.  At first I doubted this, but thanks to
     Jason's buffered message to me, figuring out the problem
     was trivial.  Using "DEBUG" mode, Jason buffered a session in DS-DOS
     in which he ATTRIB'd and DEL'd files.

     The problem was that DS-DOS wouldn't touch files if they didn't have
     the "A"rchive flag set.  This problem has been fixed for the DEL, COPY,
     XCOPY, MOVE, and REName command.  Please let me know if it happens
     again, and thanks a lot, Jason!
   . There was also a message file problem, sorry about that! Fixed now!


  Version 1.8á - June 21, 1996
   . Added DS-DOS exit message by special request!
   . Improved the speed of directory recursion by 100%!  Improved
     the recursion routine to hold a larger amount of directories,
     without using up any extra memory! Commands such as DIR/S
     and CHKDSK should run twice as fast, now!

  Version 1.9á - July 8, 1996
   . Fixed a problem with recursion.  Commands such as DIR *.MSG failed
     because I forgot to check that aspect of recursing. :(
     Fixed now! :)

  Version 2.0 - August 14, 1996
   . Now you can give users access to specific directories in DS-DOS!
     I built in a full user add/edit routine so you can add users, and
     specify a directory for them to start in.  From here,  you can
     set which commands they have access to in the full screen data
     entry session.  You may also set it so that the user may NOT
     move to a parent directory of the directory that he/she is in.

     For example, if you have multi-homed web-sites, and you want to
     give a user access to his web-site, you can set his home directory
     to be:

     c:\wgserv\webpages\user

     and set it so he can't move to any previous directory.  He can
     although navigate within his home directory, and create/remove
     subdirectories under his directory (assuming he has access to the
     MD and RD commands).

   . All user accounts have passwords for security reasons, each password
     is specific to the user.
   . Added the "edituser", "deluser", and "users" commands to the DS-DOS
     shell.  Check them out!!
   . Added CNF Level 4 option "AIDTXFER" which allows you to record
     all upload/downloads from the shell to the Audit trail.
   . Added CNF Level 4 option "BATCHPRT" for batch file protection.  With
     this option enabled, batch files will not run unless the first line
     of the batch file has the token "DIALDOS" in it.  This is so people
     don't run batch files by accident, etc.
   . I am now using a new library in place of the old one I was using.
     This should have no side effects, it should actually be more
     efficient, but I doubt any difference will be noticed.
   . All bugs related to: dir /s  have been fixed.


  Version 3.0 - October 14, 1996
   . DS-DOS is the first NO MSG compliant module released from DialSoft.
     All options previously found in LEVEL3 and LEVEL4 CNF are now moved
     online for YOUR convenience.
   . Fixed a problem with the editor, always added a blank first line to all
     saved files.  Now works correctly.

  Version 3.1 - October 20, 1996
   . Fixed a wierd issue where the "DELTREE" command would hang you up
     if you deleted a large directory.

  Version 3.2 - October 28, 1996
   . Fixed problem with changing a users home directory.

  Version 3.4 - February 2, 1997
   . Fixed infamous "CD" and "RD" problems with empty directories on Novell
     servers!
   . Fixed a problem with invalid directories for users.

  Version 3.5 - February 12, 1997
   . Added "colors".  Now you can modify your directory listings with COLOR!
     You can specify a color for all directories, as well as having 20
     different colors for filenames by association colors with the extension
     of the filename!  Each user can have their own color settings!

  Version 3.53 - April 15, 1997
   . Fixed stupid xcopy /s bug.  My fault! Pttth!

  Version 3.6 - May 14, 1997
   . Added GREP command!  I needed it badly for a couple projects, and I
     think it is a GREAT addition to this package.
   . You may also pipe to grep in certain situations!

     For example:

       dir | grep 05-13-97           - will list all files with todays date!

     You can get even fancier and do this:

       dir | grep 05-13-97 | more    - to get --more-- pauses!


     Note: Before you can use the GREP command, be sure to EDITUSER yourself
           to enable your access to that command!  By default, all newly
           added commands are disabled for ALL users.  Type HELP GREP online
           for more info!

     Note #2: The speed of the grep command is controlled by the SPEED
              option which controls the speed of copying as well.  A higher
              speed value will increase performance,  but it may also
              slow down your system response time.  The default value of
              50 should be sufficient.

   . Fixed "edituser" bug where if you entered a handle with a space in it,
     it would load the wrong user.
   . Aliased some basic commands with unix commands.  This was requested
     by someone who wanted this support, these commands are only documented
     here:

       DOS        Unix equivalent
       ---------------------------
       dir        ls
       copy       cp
       xcopy      xcp
       move       mv
       del        rm
       rd         rmdir
       md         mkdir
       edit       vi
       type       cat

   . Increased DS-DOS's editor buffer to 32,000 bytes!  Now you can edit
     files upto 400 lines large!
   . Added a switch to the TYPE command.  Use type <file> /# and it will
     display line numbers with the file, so you can see how large it is.
   . Fixed a problem with entering DS-DOS for the first time, and having
     an incorrect directory.
   . Increased recursion buffer.

  Version 3.7 - June 6, 1997
   . Increased overall performance, 500% speed increase! whooooooosh!
   . Fixed problem with HISTORY command.
   . Fixed small | GREP problem.

  Version 3.8 - July 9, 1997
   . WorldGroup 3.0 port.

  Version 3.9.0 - November 12, 2026
   . Ported to The Major BBS v10 by Mark Laudenbach.
   . Activation codes and the 14-day demo are gone; the module is fully
     enabled.  The activation code field is removed from "config".
   . Removed the install-day e-mail that sent the BBS's name, address and
     phone numbers to DialSoft.
   . ATTRIB +A/-A, +H/-H, +R/-R and +S/-S change file attributes again
     (the Windows NT build ignored them).
   . TIME shows the actual current time (the Windows NT build did not).
   . DIR and CHKDSK report free space in bytes, correctly on large drives.
   . Large numbers get their commas in the right places (1,234,567).
   . The COPY percentage meter works on files over 21 MB.
   . Long Windows file names no longer overrun internal buffers in COPY,
     MOVE, XCOPY /S, DIR /S, DELTREE or file downloads.
   . A BBS with no PROMPT environment variable gives new DS-DOS users the
     MS-DOS default prompt ($p$g) instead of failing.
   . $H in a PROMPT is a backspace (it printed an "8").
   . A long file name typed as an argument is refused instead of being
     cut to 8.3 (DEL REPORT_2024_OLD.DOC could delete REPORT_2.DOC).
     Paths too long for DS-DOS are skipped or refused, never cut short.
   . MOVE keeps the source file if the copy fails (e.g. a full disk).
   . EDIT refuses a file too large for the editor instead of opening
     part of it (saving used to lose the rest of the file).
   . Users kept in a home directory can no longer reach a directory
     whose name merely starts the same way.
   . DEBUG needs the sysop key.
   . A caller who drops no longer leaves files open or EDIT locked.
   . DIR and CHKDSK totals are correct past 2 GB.
   . CHKDSK no longer counts directories left from an aborted DIR /S.
